# CODEOWNERS — automatic review routing.
#
# Every line assigns reviewers to a path pattern. The LAST matching pattern wins,
# so the most specific rules are at the bottom.
#
# A handle here MUST resolve to a user with write access to this repository.
# GitHub silently ignores an owner it cannot resolve: no review is requested,
# nothing warns you, and the gap only surfaces when a change that needed a
# second reviewer merges without one. That has already happened once in this
# repository — see the commit that replaced a non-existent handle.
#
# Verify after editing:
#   gh api repos/rednavis/distributed-lock-lab/collaborators/<handle>
#
# Update this file in the same pull request that changes MAINTAINERS.md.
# See GOVERNANCE.md §2 for how many approvals each kind of change needs.

# Default owner for everything not matched below.
*                                   @nirmata78

# --- Two-maintainer surfaces -------------------------------------------------
# GOVERNANCE.md §2 requires TWO approvals for these. Branch protection cannot
# express a per-path approval count, so it enforces one approval globally and
# the second is a convention the reviewers uphold. Listing both owners here is
# what makes that convention mechanically possible: either can supply the
# review, and both are notified.

# Authoritative contracts. A change here is breaking by definition and needs an
# amendment row in docs/04-contracts.md §4.5. Never amended silently in a PR.
